# Runtime state and third-party blobs — never part of the tree.
#
# `data/atlas.db` is the archive; `data/requests.db` is the queue the window writes
# and the agent claims, in its own file so the window never has a writable handle on
# the archive. Both are per-machine, created on first run, and neither is source.
# The `data/*.db-wal` and `data/*.db-shm` lines are SQLite's own siblings of either
# database; the rest is what a run leaves behind (logs, the pid lock, the page cache,
# the browser profile).
data/*.db
data/*.db-wal
data/*.db-shm
data/*.lock
data/*.log
data/cache/
data/chrome-profile/
# The installer's own records and per-machine user settings (never source).
data/setup.json
data/license.json
data/config.json
data/agent-status.json
data/agent-status.json.tmp

# The model weights. 8 GB of GGUF is not source: `atlas setup` fetches the file the
# README names, into this directory, on the machine that will run it. Nothing here is
# committed, and the repository stays small enough to clone in a second.
models/*
!models/.gitkeep

# Built artifacts: the Rust target tree, the model server built from the vendored tree,
# the window's assets generated at build time.
rust/target/
bin/
tools/llama.cpp/build/
tools/llama.cpp/*.log
tools/llama.cpp/models/
# The wasm frontend is a *build product* (`rust/build-ui.sh` writes it): 11 MB of
# generated JavaScript, wasm and tiles that no shipped binary reads any more — the app
# is the native window. The vendored geo data next to it (`public/vendor/`) is source
# and stays.
public/ui/

# Editors and the OS.
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
